of primary pulmonary risk score and a second pulmonary risk score each computed using distinct signal metrics. Such composite pulmonary risk score can be used for detecting a pulmonary disease such as COPD, or for determining a composite health status of the patient. (Application atSVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.150.15[0056], [0063], and [0067].) As described in Applicant's specification, pulmonary disease detection may be turned off or temporarily withhold if the patient has a low pulmonary risk, and turned back on when the patient has a high pulmonary risk. (See Application at   [0095] and FIG. 6.) Table 1 further shows examples of various pulmonary disease detection algorithms corresponding to different pulmonary risk levels. Accordingly, a more accurate composite pulmonary risk assessment, such as in accordance with the claimed invention, would lead to improved accuracy of risk-based pulmonary disease detection. Furthermore, because pulmonary disease detection can be turned on when a high pulmonary risk is indicated, using the pulmonary risk assessment as described in Applicant's specification can help save battery power and conserve computing resources, thereby improving the functionality of the medical-device system.

The Office Action asserts that elements 433 and a 443 in Patangay FIG. 4 amount to a teaching of the primary and secondary pulmonary risk scores as claimed. (Office Action at 4.) Applicant respectfully disagrees. Element 433 is "a single dimension thoracic fluid alert," represented by a classification of "dry", "wet", or "intermediate state" of thoracic fluid. (Patangay atSVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.150.077[0053].) Element 443 is "a single dimension respiratory function alert," represented by aclassification of "normal", "respiratory distress", or "intermediate state" of respiratory function. (Id. at   [0055].) Such single dimension alerts or categorical classifications are clearly not risk scores each taking a numerical value, as required by claim 1.
